% Runtime registration: what an application adds to the map at startup. The
% runner calls Map::reset() before every case, so nothing here leaks.

### name: register.define.adds-a-function
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "CRC32", {"tpl": "MY_CRC({0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
CRC32("x")
--- expect
MY_CRC('x')
===
### name: register.define.withdraws-with-a-reason
--- note
The reason reaches the caller as the message, exactly as a reason authored in
the JSON does. Same mechanism, no second path.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", "this deployment forbids case folding in SQL"]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: register.define.on-a-base-reaches-every-leaf
--- note
The precedence rule Map::entry exists to get right. The generated tables are
pre-flattened, so consulting them per level would let mariadb's own inherited
UPPER shadow an entry registered against ansi — and registering against a base
is documented to reach everything below it.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["ansi", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "ANSI_UPPER({0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- expect
ANSI_UPPER('a')
===
### name: register.define.leaf-still-wins-over-a-base
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["ansi", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "ANSI_UPPER({0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]},
 {"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "LEAF_UPPER({0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- expect
LEAF_UPPER('a')
===
### name: register.define.an-operator
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "ops", "&", {"variants": {"text": "({0} || {1})"}, "ret": "@concat"}]}]
--- source
"a" & "b"
--- expect
('a' || 'b')
===
### name: register.dialect.escapes-apply-longest-first
--- note
Escaping is a single left-to-right pass with the longest rule tried first, so
a two-character rule fires before the one-character rule that is its prefix.
No shipped dialect has a multi-character escape key -- mariadb maps ' and \\,
both one character -- so the rule is a no-op on shipped data and nothing
asserted it until a mutation reversing the sort walked through the whole
suite. A registered dialect is the only place it can be seen.
--- dialect
longest
--- register
[{"dialect": "longest", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5",
  "lexical": {"textEscape": {"ab": "<AB>", "a": "<A>"}}}]
--- source
"ab"
--- expect
'<AB>'
===
### name: register.define.lexical-references-expand-at-run-time
--- note
The generator expands these for the shipped map. An application writing a
template never passes through the generator and gets the same vocabulary.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "LEN", {"tpl": "LEN({0}{textCollate})", "ret": "NUM"}]}]
--- source
LEN("a")
--- expect
LEN('a' COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: register.skel.withdrawn-refuses-before-the-operands
--- note
A skeleton the dialect cannot express is checked BEFORE the expression's own
operands are rendered, so the answer names the dialect gap rather than
whatever the first operand happens to be wrong about. Python and PHP get the
order from left-to-right argument evaluation -- `_fill_named(_skeleton(...),
_slots(...))` -- and a host that builds the slot map as its own statement
first reverses it, answering E_SQL_UNBOUND here instead. No shipped dialect
withdraws inRelation, so only a registration can ask.
--- dialect
norel
--- register
[{"dialect": "norel", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5"},
 {"define": ["norel", "skel", "inRelation", null]}]
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "t", "alias": "oi", "scalar": "sku",
           "fields": {"sku": {"column": "sku", "table": "oi", "type": "TEXT"}},
           "correlate": {"raw": "oi.o = o.id"}}}
--- source
UNBOUND IN ITEMS
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:9
===
### name: register.since.gates-on-the-target-version
--- note
mariadb declares 10.5, so an entry needing 99.0 is refused. The version
compared is always the target's, never a base's.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "NEW_UPPER({0})", "ret": "TEXT", "since": "99.0"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- error
E_SQL_DIALECT 1:1
===
### name: register.dialect.a-newer-version-passes-the-same-gate
--- note
This is the whole mechanism for "keep maps for different versions": a version
is another link in the chain and needs no special code anywhere.
--- dialect
mariadb-11.8
--- register
[{"dialect": "mariadb-11.8", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "11.8"},
 {"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "NEW_UPPER({0})", "ret": "TEXT", "since": "11.0"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- expect
NEW_UPPER('a')
===
### name: register.dialect.inherits-everything-else
--- dialect
mariadb-11.8
--- register
[{"dialect": "mariadb-11.8", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "11.8"}]
--- source
"A" $== "a"
--- expect
(CAST('A' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_bin)
===
### name: register.dialect.may-override-a-lexical-key
--- dialect
mariadb-nocollate
--- register
[{"dialect": "mariadb-nocollate", "extends": "mariadb", "version": "10.5",
  "lexical": {"textCollate": " COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin"}}]
--- source
"A" $== "a"
--- expect
(CAST('A' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin = CAST('a' AS CHAR) COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_bin)
===
### name: register.dialect.unknown-parent-is-a-programming-error
--- note
Deliberately not an E_SQL_ code. Registering against a dialect that does not
exist is a mistake in the application's startup, not a rule that cannot be
translated — and the difference matters, because tryTranslate() swallows the
second and must not swallow the first. Sel\Registry::define draws the same line
for a duplicate function.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"dialect": "wishful", "extends": "nosuchdialect", "version": "1.0"}]
--- source
1 + 1
--- throws
LogicException
===
### name: register.define.unknown-section-is-a-programming-error
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "nonesuch", "UPPER", null]}]
--- source
1 + 1
--- throws
LogicException
